  "executive_briefing": "The corporate and international tax landscape in late 2026 is experiencing structural adjustments driven by systemic implementation of multilateral agreements, digital tax administrative enforcement, and volatile tariff regimes. Over 60 countries have officially enacted or are actively implementing the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax rules (GloBE), altering tax planning for multinational enterprises. While statutory rates shift incrementally, average effective tax rates (EATR) stand globally at 20.5%, balancing national competitiveness with new base erosion baselines. Concurrently, digital tax compliance transitions from policy to practice as seen with Singapore's phased e-invoicing InvoiceNow network rollout running through 2031. Meanwhile, trade policy remains extremely volatile, with major economies executing reciprocal tariffs\u2014such as the Trump 2.0 baseline measures and retaliatory restrictions between the US and Canada\u2014tempered by ongoing legal challenges regarding the administrative scope of executive-imposed import fees.",
